From 68ddb3521d02e0166e233296b4cda3a8ec3dcbd4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 15:19:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: play Dolby Vision that comes in two video tracks

A UHD Blu-ray carries Dolby Vision profile 7 in two video tracks of
its transport stream: the base layer (HDR10) and the enhancement layer
with the RPU. The player opened the first video track and played
HDR10, on every GPU (hardware test of 2026-10-03, a .m2ts file).

libavformat already puts the two tracks in a stream group: by the
Dolby Vision descriptor of the enhancement track, or, for the discs
themselves, which have no descriptor, by the stream numbers of the
Blu-ray format. Everything after the demuxer takes the one-track form
that Matroska files carry (after the units of the base layer of an
access unit come the units of the enhancement layer, each inside a
unit of type 63, and the RPU as it is), so the demuxer now makes that
form while it reads:

- the enhancement track is no stream of the player, and the base
  track is the Dolby Vision stream, with the record of the one-track
  form (for a disc: profile 7, the level of its size and rate, both
  layers and the RPU, HDR10 base);
- CDVLayerMerge pairs the packets of the two tracks by decoding time,
  which is what the two tracks share. An access unit that has no time
  of its own (a PES packet can hold more than one) has the time that
  follows from the unit before it in its track, as the standard says;
- a base packet waits for its enhancement packet and goes out alone
  when the enhancement layer has none for it. How far apart a disc
  puts the two is not known, so the wait has no short fixed length:
  it ends when the enhancement layer is past the packet, when it is
  silent (no packet of it for 2.5 seconds of base packets: nothing
  then waits until it is back), when 240 base packets wait, or at the
  end of the stream;
- the log says which pair was found and how, and, after 600 access
  units and at the end, how many were joined, how many went out alone
  and how many packets waited at most.

The presentation times of the two layers of an access unit need not be
the same: the layers can be coded with different picture orders, as
they are in the test file. The enhancement picture is already paired
with its base picture by picture order; the RPU is the subject of the
next change.

Only the start-code streams of a transport stream are joined. A pair
in a Matroska or MP4 file (length-prefixed units, parameter sets out
of band) is left as it was. A second HEVC track of a Blu-ray stream
that has not the size of an enhancement layer is not joined either,
and the log says so.

A stream that is declared this way and has no RPUs plays as before:
the decoder already returns to the ordinary output when pictures come
without their own RPU.

Finding the pair, reading and joining are in a file that does not
depend on Kodi, and dvmerge_test.cpp runs that file with FFmpeg 9.0.2
outside of it: the two tracks of a profile 7 FEL test file against the
one-track Matroska file made of the same two tracks (2878 access
units with the same units and the same bytes and the same record, but
for the last enhancement unit, which has no time of its own and goes
with the base unit that its place in the track gives it, where that
file has it on the last one); the same with a copy of the file
signalled as the discs are (stream type 0x24, no descriptor; only its
program map tables differ); after seeks; with packets that are
missing, late, far behind or far ahead, or without a time; and with
buffers that are not unit streams. Not tested: a disc, a disc image
or a BDMV folder, and playback itself, where the start-code form of
the joined stream meets the decoder for the first time.
